==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*neman}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*nemaną}}, from {{der|ang|ine-pro|*nem-}}.

Germanic cognates include {{cog|ofs|nima}}, {{cog|osx|niman}}, {{cog|odt|neman}}, {{cog|goh|neman}}, {{cog|non|nema}}, {{cog|got|𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root was also the source of {{cog|sga|nem||present}}, {{cog|lv|ņemt||to take}}, {{cog|sq|nëm||to take, curse}}, {{cog|grc|νέμω}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|niman}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# to [[take]]
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://archive.org/details/kingalfredsorosi79oros/page/158/mode/2up translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius’]] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: {{quote|ang|''⁊ hæfdon ġeleornad mā cræfta hū hīe þā elpendas beswīcan mehton, mid þǣm þæt hīe '''nāmon''' trēowu, ⁊ slōgon on oþerne ende moniġe sċearpe īsene næġlas, ⁊ hīe mid flexe bewundon, ⁊ onbǣrndon hit, ⁊ beþyddan hit þonne on þone elpend hindan...''|And they had learned more tricks through which they could ensnare the elephants; they '''took''' stakes and drove into the other end many sharp iron nails, and wrapped them in flax, and set them on fire, and then thrust them into the elephant from behind...}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n83/mode/2up Mark 2:10-11]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Hē cwæð tō þām laman, þē iċ seċġe, arīs, '''nim''' ðīn bed, and gā tō þīnum hūse.''|He said to the lame man, "I say to you, arise, '''take''' your bed, and go to your house."}}

